LSI Logic Fusion-MPT SAS (SAS1068) miniport, PCI 1000:0054 (the VMware "LSI Logic SAS" controller; also
emulated by QEMU: -device mptsas1068)

The driver (mptsas.c) is new code, GPL-2.0-or-later, written against the legacy SCSI port
interface (scsiport) like the BusLogic and PVSCSI miniports.

Origin of the device interface
  Project:  OpenBSD
  Files:    sys/dev/ic/mpireg.h revision 1.45 (2014/03/25) and sys/dev/ic/mpi.c revision 1.226
            (2023/07/06): the initialisation sequence only
  URL:      https://github.com/openbsd/src/blob/master/sys/dev/ic/mpireg.h
  License:  ISC, notice kept in the header of mptsas.c
  Used:     the register offsets, the doorbell and reply queue bits, the function numbers, the layouts of
            the IOC facts, IOC init, port facts, port enable and SCSI I/O messages and of the
            scatter/gather elements, and the order of the start-up steps (doorbell handshake for IOC facts
            and IOC init, reply frames, port facts, port enable). No code was copied: the OpenBSD driver is
            written for its own SCSI midlayer and DMA API.

Not used: the Linux drivers mptbase/mptsas/mpt3sas (GPL-2.0-only, not compatible with this tree), and any
LSI or VMware binary driver.

What it does not do: events (no hot plug of disks), configuration pages (no RAID volume handling, no
transfer parameters), MSI, more than one logical unit per target, CDBs longer than 16 bytes, more than
35 scatter/gather elements per request, the parallel SCSI chip LSI 1030 (PCI 1000:0030: not emulated by
QEMU, so not tried). The sizes that the controller reports (request frame, reply queue depth, credits,
number of devices) are checked before they are used, every wait on the controller is bounded, and every
address and context that the controller writes into a reply is validated.
